.TH "setup-home-server" "8" "2026-09-11" "ltsp6"
.SH "NAME"
setup\-home\-server \(em Export home directories over NFS to the application servers
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
\fBsetup\-home\-server\fR \fIdomain\fR \fIrealm\fR \fIadminuser\fR \fIadminpass\fR \fIexport\-network\fR
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
Run on the file server, as root. Joins the domain, exports /srv/home over NFS
with \fBno_root_squash\fR so that pam_mkhomedir(8) on the application servers
can create directories, and configures sssd with the same identity mapping the
application servers use.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.PP
.IP "\fIdomain\fP" 4
Lower\-case domain, for example ltsplab.local.
.IP "\fIrealm\fP" 4
Upper\-case Kerberos realm, for example LTSPLAB.LOCAL.
.IP "\fIadminuser\fP \fIadminpass\fP" 4
Credentials used once, for the domain join.
.IP "\fIexport\-network\fP" 4
Who may mount the export, for example 172.17.240.0/20.
.SH "NOTES"
.PP
The point of the sssd configuration here is \fBldap_id_mapping = True\fR,
identical to the application servers. Every member then derives the same UID
from the same AD SID, so a home directory served to two servers has one owner
rather than two. Get this wrong and the failure is not a mount error but files
that belong to nobody on half the fleet.
.PP
Verify across hosts before trusting it:
.PP
.nf
id test1            # same uid on every application server and here
touch /home/test1/.probe    # on one server
ls /home/test1/.probe       # visible on the others and here
.fi
.PP
An unprivileged LXC container cannot run the kernel NFS server. If the file
server is a container, it has to be a privileged one.
.PP
The mount on the client side is \fBhard\fR on purpose. A \fBsoft\fR mount turns
a server outage into short writes and silently damaged files in people's home
directories; \fBhard\fR turns it into processes that wait. Know what that
costs: while the server is unreachable every login on an application server
blocks in the kernel, sshd included, and the machine looks dead from outside.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
\fBsetup\-ltsp\-app\fP(8),
\fBprovision\-samba\-ad\-dc\fP(8),
\fBexports\fP(5),
\fBsssd.conf\fP(5),
\fBpam_mkhomedir\fP(8).
.SH "AVAILABILITY"
.PP
\fBsetup\-home\-server\fR is part of the \fBltsp\-server\fP package; the latest
version is available from \fIhttps://github.com/MAButz/ltsp6\fR.
